# Plan documentation policy

Before starting any non-trivial task — anything that touches multiple
files, changes project configuration (Dockerfile, devcontainer.json,
next.config.js, CI, auth, build tooling), or involves more than a couple
of steps — create a plan file at:

`docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-short-slug.md`

Use the current date and a short kebab-case description of the task
(e.g. `2026-08-01-playwright-e2e-setup.md`). Skip this for trivial changes
(typo fixes, small copy edits, single-line tweaks).

## Plan file structure

```md
# <Task title>

## Goal
What this task is trying to accomplish and why.

## Plan
The proposed approach, as a short numbered list or steps.
Note any alternatives considered and why they were rejected, if relevant.

## Outcome
(Fill in after execution)
What actually happened. Call out anything that diverged from the plan —
unexpected errors, workarounds, follow-up items. This section is often
more useful later than the plan itself.
```

## Rules

- Write the `Goal` and `Plan` sections *before* making changes, not
  retroactively.
- After the task is done (or abandoned), append the `Outcome` section
  to the same file — don't skip this even if the task succeeded exactly
  as planned; a short "went as expected" is still useful signal.
- Never delete or overwrite a previous plan file. Each task gets its own
  file, permanently kept for history.
- After adding a new plan file, add or update a one-line entry in
  `docs/plans/README.md` (create it if missing) linking to the file, so
  past plans can be scanned without opening each one individually.
- Plan files are committed to git, not gitignored.
